wlcs v1.8.1: BadBufferTest.test_truncated_shm_file puts its ftruncate() inside an assert().

The test makes a buffer from a pool and then truncates the pool's file, so the compositor's read of the buffer is a bus
error it has to turn into `wl_shm.invalid_fd`. The truncation was written as `assert(ftruncate(fd, 12) == 0)`.
scripts/wlcs.sh builds wlcs in Release, which defines NDEBUG and compiles assert() away, the call with it: the file was
never truncated, so no compositor could raise the error and the test failed for every one. The call now runs and the
result is asserted.

Applied by scripts/wlcs.sh with `git apply`; drop it when wlcs fixes the test upstream.

diff --git a/tests/test_bad_buffer.cpp b/tests/test_bad_buffer.cpp
index 8e77938..f6b4a79 100644
--- a/tests/test_bad_buffer.cpp
+++ b/tests/test_bad_buffer.cpp
@@ -59,7 +59,11 @@ create_bad_shm_buffer(wlcs::Client& client, int width, int height)
 	/* Truncate the file to a small size, so that the compositor
 	 * will access it out-of-bounds, and hit SIGBUS.
 	 */
-	assert(ftruncate(fd, 12) == 0);
+	/* The truncation is the point of the test, so it is not inside the assert: wlcs builds in Release, which compiles
+	 * assert() away and the ftruncate() with it, and the test then tests nothing. */
+	int const truncated = ftruncate(fd, 12);
+	assert(truncated == 0);
+	(void)truncated;
 	close(fd);
 
 	return buffer;
